 ## Background
-Originated in SVRecon [S1, unofficial] and MonoSDF [S2] as a method to leverage monocular geometric cues for neural implicit surface reconstruction. SVRecon introduced the use of PI3 point maps for normal priors with a specific annealing schedule [S1, unofficial]. MonoSDF proposed a similar concept using angular and L1 losses [S2]. The method addresses the limitation of relying solely on photometric and depth losses, which can be ambiguous for surface orientation [S1, unofficial].
+Originated in SVRecon [S1, unofficial] and MonoSDF [S3] as a method to leverage monocular geometric cues for neural implicit surface reconstruction. SVRecon introduced the use of PI3 point maps for normal priors with a specific annealing schedule [S1, unofficial]. MonoSDF proposed a similar concept using angular and L1 losses [S3]. The method addresses the limitation of relying solely on photometric and depth losses, which can be ambiguous for surface orientation [S1, unofficial].
 
